I cannot reproduce the reported outage. Layer-3 connectivity between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and the partner host pc2 (200.1.1.2) is healthy end-to-end. Evidence: topology-wide reachability is 0% loss both directions, and targeted ping tests from pc1 to 200.1.1.2 and back each delivered 10/10 replies with ~1 ms RTT. Host routing is correct (pc1 default via 195.11.14.1; pc2 default via 200.1.1.1). On the routers, forwarding and control-plane are normal: router1 and router2 have the expected connected routes and BGP-learned routes for each other’s LANs, and the BGP session is Established with prefixes exchanged (router1 sees 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 sees 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). This explains why pings work consistently now.

Localization: No faults on router1:eth1←→pc1:eth0, router2:eth1←→pc2:eth0, or the inter-router link router1:eth0↔router2:eth0. Control-plane (FRR/BGP) is up on both routers.

Root cause: No network-layer fault was found; the path is currently operating normally. If the user’s “partner system” refers to an application service, note that pc2 has no TCP services listening (netstat shows only 127.0.0.11 for resolver), which would cause application connection failures despite working IP reachability.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Ask the partner/application owner to verify and start the required service on pc2 (200.1.1.2) and/or its firewall policy. If the issue recurs at the network layer, provide exact protocol/port and timestamp so we can correlate, but at present the network is healthy. 
